    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey my tomato plants are looking kind of sickly. the leaves on the bottom are turning yellow and falling off. what do you think is going on?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sickly tomato plants can be a real nuisance, especially when you're expecting a bountiful harvest. It's not uncommon for the lower leaves to turn yellow and drop off, a process known as abscission. It's like when a youngling orcs in the goblin clans shed their baby teeth - it's a natural part of growth, but it can be alarming to see it happening to your plants. \n\nHowever, there could be other factors at play. Overwatering, for instance, can cause root rot, which can lead to yellowing leaves and a general decline in plant health. It's a bit like when a goblin's lair gets too damp and moldy - it's a perfect breeding ground for all sorts of nasty critters. \n\nTo diagnose the issue, I'd recommend checking the soil moisture and pH levels. If the soil is too wet, it might be causing the roots to rot. And if the pH is off, it could be affecting nutrient uptake. You might also want to inspect the plant for any signs of pests or diseases. A goblin's love of shiny objects can sometimes lead to a fondness for snacking on plant sap, but I'm sure that's not the case",
