    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin lore, didn't I? Just forget about the orcs and goblin clans, and let's focus on your tomatoes.\n\nTo adjust the pH, you'll need to test the soil and determine its current level. Most tomato plants prefer a slightly acidic to neutral soil pH, ranging from 6.0 to 7.0. If your soil is too alkaline (high pH), it can lead to nutrient deficiencies, especially for iron and magnesium. On the other hand, if it's too acidic, it can cause nutrient imbalances and affect nutrient availability.\n\nTo adjust the pH, you can use lime to raise it or sulfur to lower it. It's a bit like how goblins might use different types of traps to catch their prey - you need to choose the right tool for the job. For example, if your soil is too acidic, you might use dolomitic limestone to raise the pH, while sulfur would be better suited for lowering it.\n\nWhen using lime or sulfur, it's essential to follow the recommended application rates to avoid over-correcting. You can also consider getting a soil test kit to get a more accurate reading of your soil's pH level. And, just",
